# tools/encoding_base58_witness.rish — Base58 (Bitcoin alphabet) authored in pure Rye: the binary-to-text armor an address, a public key,
# or a content id wears when a human must read, type, or copy it, proven against the canonical Bitcoin Core known-answers and byte-for-byte
# against an independent u128-integer implementation (encoding · the serialization sibling of Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: encoding — the text-form sibling of the crypto library, beside encoding/base64.rye. Where crypto/ authors the mathematics that
# produces bytes, encoding/ authors the text those bytes travel in, keeping the same parity discipline: authored from the standard, calling
# no std codec in the primitive, proven byte-for-byte against a reference runnable now.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (the wave that seats the Rye-first spine).
# Clean-room: the 58-character alphabet and the leading-zero law are Bitcoin's own public convention; the arithmetic is ours
# (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# encoding/base58.rye authors Base58 from Bitcoin's public convention — the 58-symbol alphabet (no 0/O/I/l), the leading-'1' law for zero
# bytes, the base-256-to-base-58 long division and its inverse. The primitive calls no std base58 (Zig ships none). Parity is proven at
# selftest against two references runnable now: the canonical Bitcoin Core encode/decode known-answers (including the leading-zero and
# all-zero cases) match text for text and round-trip back, and a second, INDEPENDENT implementation over Zig's native u128 integer matches
# byte-for-byte across payloads 0..15 bytes with a round-trip at every length. A character outside the alphabet refuses BadChar, a too-small
# destination refuses DestTooSmall. Purely local arithmetic — no key, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/encoding_base58_witness.rish

say "encoding-base58: Language EN — Base58 (Bitcoin alphabet) is the binary-to-text armor a value wears when a human is in the loop: an address on a screen, a public key on a card, a content id typed by hand. It omits the four glyphs an eye confuses — 0, O, I, l — and drops the + / and padding that break a double-click or a URL, so a value read aloud or copied by hand survives the trip. The crypto library proves the bytes; this module renders them to the alphabet a hand can carry, and recovers them exactly."
say "encoding-base58: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: the whole payload reads as one big-endian integer written in base 58, most-significant digit first, and every leading zero byte is restored as a leading '1'; decoding inverts the map so the same bytes always render to the same text and the same text always recovers the same bytes."
say "encoding-base58: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std codec (Zig ships no base58); it is authored from Bitcoin's public convention, bounded input (max_decoded_bytes, max_encoded_bytes), contract asserts on every function, buffer-length checked before the first write, long division carrying well under the u32 ceiling. Parity holds against the canonical Bitcoin Core known-answers AND a second, independent u128-integer implementation byte-for-byte with a round-trip at every length; a character outside the alphabet refuses BadChar, a too-small destination refuses DestTooSmall — each before a partial result escapes. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "encoding/bin/base58"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p encoding/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build encoding/base58.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "encoding-base58: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "encoding/bin/base58.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "encoding-base58: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "encoding-base58: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN encoding-base58' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "encoding-base58: selftest did not print the GREEN encoding-base58 line"
